/threads/sugar-free.5929344/"}]}},{"type":"openrouter:web_search","id":"st_tmp_l8e8z3p21p","status":"completed","action":{"type":"search","query":"\"I quit sugar\" blog my story personal experience","sources":[{"type":"url","url":"https://commonsenseorganics.co.nz/blog/my-i-quit-sugar-diary/"},{"type":"url","url":"https://sarahwilson.com/2011/02/sunday-life-i-quit-sugar/"},{"type":"url","url":"https://sarahwilson.com/2011/01/im-quitting-sugar-1/"},{"type":"url","url":"https://iquitsugar.com/blogs/community-stories/i-ve-lost-8-8-kilos-holly-s-weight-loss-journey-with-the-8-week-program"},{"type":"url","url":"https://addisonswonderland.com/quit-sugar/"}]}},{"id":"rs_tmp_5arskk12v6d","type":"reasoning","status":"completed","content":[{"type":"reasoning_text","text":"Good, I have some initial results. Let me now fetch the most promising pages and continue with more searches. Let me fetch several of the promising blog posts and forum threads, and continue searching for more.\n\nLet me fetch the most promising ones first:\n1. The Commonsense Organics \"I Quit Sugar Diary\" - rich long-form blog\n2. The MoneySavingExpert \"I Quit Sugar\" support thread - long forum thread\n3. The SoberRecovery sugar addiction threads\n4. The ahnest kitchen sugar free blog\n5. The Sarah Wilson I quit sugar blog\n\nAnd continue searching.Good, I have some initial results. Let me now fetch the most promising pages and continue with more searches. Let me fetch several of the promising blog posts and forum threads, and continue searching for more.\n\nLet me fetch the most promising ones first:\n1. The Commonsense Organics \"I Quit Sugar Diary\" - rich long-form blog\n2. The MoneySavingExpert \"I Quit Sugar\" support thread - long forum thread\n3. The SoberRecovery sugar addiction threads\n4. The ahnest kitchen sugar free blog\n5. The Sarah Wilson I quit sugar blog\n\nAnd continue searching."}],"summary":[]},{"type":"openrouter:web_fetch","id":"st_tmp_xwydmqkt9f","status":"completed","url":"https://commonsenseorganics.co.nz/blog/my-i-quit-sugar-diary/","content":"- Commonsense Living\n- Recipes & Food Ideas\n- Health & Diet\n- Meet our Suppliers\n\n# No Sugar-Coating: My Real-Life 'I Quit Sugar' Diary\n\n17 January 2018 at 4:40 pm\n\nI was brought up on sugar. My bones are made from Angel Delight and hokey pokey. My veins are filled with plum crumble and raspberry liquorice sticks. Sugar is my life-force. That’s why I chose to give it up. That’s right: for eight weeks in February and March 2017, I went sugar-free. What was it like? Read on to find out. No sugar-coating, I promise.\n\nDid I collapse crying, huddled into ball on the floor? Yes.\n\nDid I crack under the pressure and eat something sweet? More than once.\n\nBut first, let’s crack into the general idea behind giving up sugar. What do you actually give up when you give up sugar, and why would you even try?\n\nMe on an average day (before I quit sugar). You get the idea.\n\n### Why give up sugar?\n\nThere are plenty of reasons why people choose to give up sugar. Personally, I'm not usually pro 'diets', and don’t take eliminating food groups lightly. But when I decided to give up sugar, my reasons felt really good and body-positive. They included:\n\nMood stabilisation. I get HUGE sugar highs and lows. Perhaps going sugar-free would change this? I imagined I would reach a semi-enlightened state involving lots of benevolent smiling and no more pedestrian road rage.\n\nGreat skin. A few friends of mine have experimented with giving up sugar, and their skin looked great! They looked like they slept 15 hours a night and got placenta facials every other day. I wanted a fresh-faced placenta-facial look too.\n\nNot being addicted to sugar. Some studies have indicated that fructose might be more rewarding and more addictive than cocaine(!) Did I want my life run by a soluble carbohydrate? The jury was out.\n\nA whole new world of flavour. According to my friends who champion quitting sugar, giving up the sweet stuff led them to a whole new appreciation for existing flavours, like carrots. And I was NOT going to go hungry. There was NO WAY I was losing out on calories. My plan? Eat lots and LOTS of organic butter. (It's at least twice as delicious as non-organic butter.)\n\nWhy not? It was just eight weeks. It wasn’t a lifetime. I’ve done way harder things before. Like that time I took the wrong train while alone at night in a small village in India. Or that time I got concussed sparring with a semi-pro boxer. Or when I sang a song about crustacean mating rituals in front of 100 people while lying on top of a piano dressed as a lobster. In comparison, giving up sugar couldn’t be THAT hard, right?\n\n...right?\n\n### What DO you give up when you give up sugar?\n\nThere are a number of sugar-free programmes out there. I chose Sarah Wilson’s I Quit Sugar, mostly because my colleague Lucy already owned the book, so I didn’t have to do anything (EXCEPT LOSE MY WILL TO LIVE).\n\nI Quit Sugar is an eight week Australian programme designed to end your addiction to sugar. They suggest you cut down on sugar in the first week or two, but after that it’s hard-line: no refined sugars, no agave, no honey and NO FRUIT (more on this later). To be fair, I Quit Sugar does allow for an occasional glass of dry wine (preferably red), because most of the sugar is fermented in the wine-making process. #smallvictories\n\nThey include lots of sugar-free meal and snack suggestions and recipes (including vege options) in the I Quit Sugar book, which I found really helpful. We sell I Quit Sugar books in store, if it's not in stock, you can ask to order it in!\n\n### The FRUIT issue: Fructose vs. Glucose\n\n“No FRUIT?” was the question I kept on hearing from concerned colleagues / friends / my Dad. The main focus of the I Quit Sugar is fructose, the form of sugar contained in fruit and other things (standard table sugar is 50% glucose and 50% fructose). Here are some reasons why:\n\nFructose doesn’t make you feel full. Eating glucose naturally triggers a ‘stop eating’ signal to be sent to our brains. Fructose doesn’t do that. In fact, consuming fructose can trigger other signals that make us want to eat more.\n\nFructose stresses out your liver. Almost all fructose gets broken down in our livers, which can over-tax it. Sucrose is different: it can be broken down by cells all over our bodies\n\nFructose is twice as sweet as glucose. It's like the Captain Planet of sugar. Very addictive.\n\nSome popular sugar alternatives, like agave, are actually very high in fructose (agave is 90% fructose!) After the 8 weeks are finished, the I Quit Sugar teams recommends experimenting with low-fructose sweeteners like stevia, rice bran syrup and cassava syrup.\n\n## MY 8 WEEKS OF NO SUGAR: A DIARY\n\n### Week One: This Isn’t Too Bad\n\nI'm doing okay, I think? I do feel bitter when I can’t try our new chocolate samples at work, but my feelings of incredible virtue and vast superiority balance out the scales. I am a jellybean martyr.\n\nDifficulty level: 4/10\n\nWhat gets me through: Coffee, and'sharing' bags of Sea Salt Serious Popcorn\n\n### Week Two: The Nothing\n\nThe days drag on, grey and endless. I cry at work three out five days. It’s like I’m lost in The Nothing from The Neverending Story, and there is no candy-coloured Luckdragon out there to rescue me. Things are tough in this sugar-free wasteland.\n\nDifficulty level: 3215/10\n\nWhat gets me through (barely): The Commonsense Kitchen spelt scones, with lashings of organic butter.\n\n### Week Three: I am invincible!\n\nMy skin is looking great. I no longer have huge energy slumps during the day. It’s like I’m stuck in a real-life Instagram filter. My confidence sky-rockets and I decide that now is a good time to give up coffee too… Two days later my colleagues call an intervention. Coffee, I will never leave you again.\n\nDifficulty level: 4.5/10\n\nWhat gets me through: Coffee, Ready Salted Proper Crisps.\n\n### Week Four: All I Have to Do Is Dream…\n\nI cheat, and eat an organic grape. After a moment of panic, I decide not to worry. Instead, I turn my attention back to what’s important. Namely: baked goods. I wake up with visions of carrot cake, I dream of cupcakes all day, and I lay my head in a cushion of apple crumble at night. It's astonishing: I haven't been this focused since the last season of Game of Thrones came out.\n\nDifficulty level: 6.8/10\n\nWhat gets me through: Coffee, Mac Snack’s Golden Roasted Macadamia Nuts\n\n### Week Five: cake for breakfast\n\nHave you ever tried Dovedale’s Paleo Turmeric Bread? It sounds like every food fad in the world baked in a pie, but it's actually 100% delicious. The coconut flour sweetness means that it’s basically like eating cake for breakfast. This week is GOOD. I seem to have escaped from the riptide of sugar addiction. In fact, instead of fantasising about bathing in a tub of cream cheese icing, I dream vividly about eating a Beurre Bosc pear. It’s flesh is buttery soft, perfectly ripe, and my teeth sink into it like a hot knife through butter. I wake feeling confused and full of remorse.\n\nDifficulty level: 3.5/10\n\nWhat gets me through: Coffee, Dovedale's Paleo Turmeric Bread\n\n### Week Six: worst birthday ever?\n\nIt’s my birthday this week. Great. I guess I’ll welcome my 32nd year by sticking a candle in a potato. Maybe I can flavour it with the salt from my tears... Actually, my birthday turns out to be okay. I don’t really miss the cake. Although I must admit that I do go a bit above my weekly wine quota…\n\nDifficulty level: 5/10\n\nWhat gets me through: Coffee, a few glasses of Brightside Pinot Gris. And, two birthday cocktails (!!!)\n\n### Week Seven: I am the Clint Eastwood of I Quit Sugar.\n\nI’m starting to feel like a grizzled Clint Eastwood of I Quit Sugar: experienced, care-worn, a veteran. It’s my new normal. Boring, even. I don’t get cravings, but I don’t feel any kind of smug satisfaction either… I guess this is my new life now.\n\nDifficulty level: 1.5/10\n\nWhat gets me through: Coffee, Raglan Coconut Yoghurt's Natural Coconut Yoghurt\n\n### Week Eight: I did it!\n\nI'm at the end of my I Quit Sugar experiment, and, to be honest, I’m feeling pretty good.\n\nDifficulty level: 1/10\n\nWhat gets me through: Coffee (always).\n\n## My 'I Quit Sugar' Verdict\n\nI had expected to feel joyful AND smug when my eight weeks were up, but things turn out a bit differently. Life is strange.\n\nI have I Quit Sugar permission to start back on the sweet stuff on Wednesday, but I ignore this and carry on for two days, without sugar, until Friday, when I eat a toasted hot cross bun. What is going on?!\n\nThat evening, I go out for a delicious sugar-laden Thai meal with my best friend. It is DELICIOUS. But waking up the next morning, I don't feel that good. I start biting my fingernails again. My skin goes has transformed from pretty clear to inflamed in less than ten hours. Insatiable cravings start. I have been back on sugar for less than a day, but I already feel like Cruella de Vil. I'm angry and bitter and I want a metaphorical coat made of metaphorical puppies.\n\nI didn't expect this to happen, but I kind of want to quit sugar... forever?! And, without sugar-cravings prompting my every food choice, it feels absolutely possible to do so. I can't believe I'm writing this, but quitting sugar has made me feel... well... free.\n\n## Resources & Further Reading:\n\n- That Sugar Film\n- The I Quit Sugar Website\n\nLiz Willoughby-Martin was the Communications Coordinator at Commonsense 2016-2017. She wrote many of our Facebook posts, our e-newsletter and blog posts.\n\n## Share\n\n## Page Links\n\n- https://commonsenseorganics.co.nz/\n- https://commonsenseorganics.co.nz/shop-online/\n- https://commonsenseorganics.co.nz/about/\n- https://commonsenseorganics.co.nz/blog/\n- https://commonsenseorganics.co.nz/stores/\n- https://commonsenseorganics.co.nz/blog/category/commonsense-living/\n- https://commonsenseorganics.co.nz/blog/category/recipes-and-food-ideas/\n- https://commonsenseorganics.co.nz/blog/category/health-and-diet/\n- https://commonsenseorganics.co.nz/blog/category/meet-our-suppliers/\n- https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/23719144\n- https://iquitsugar.com/\n- https://iquitsugar.com/shop/complete-plan-and-recipe-book/\n- https://commonsenseorganics.co.nz/blog/my-i-quit-sugar-diary/home/SearchForm?Search=stevia&amp;action_results=Go\n- https://commonsenseorganics.co.nz/blog/my-i-quit-sugar-diary/shop-online/groceries/chips-and-crackers/sea-salt-serious-popcorn/\n- https://commonsenseorganics.co.nz/blog/my-i-quit-sugar-diary/shop-online/groceries/chips-and-crackers/hand-cooked-crisps/\n- https://commonsenseorganics.co.nz/shop-online/groceries/dried-fruit-nuts-and-seeds/mac-snack-golden-macadamia-nuts/\n- https://commonsenseorganics.co.nz/blog/my-i-quit-sugar-diary/shop-online/fridge-foods-and-dairy/yoghurt/raglan-coconut-yoghurt/\n- http://thatsugarfilm.com/\n- https://commonsenseorganics.co.nz/shop-online/what-you-need-to-know/\n- https://commonsenseorganics.co.nz/privacy-policy/\n","title":"Commonsense - No Sugar-Coating: My Real-Life 'I Quit Sugar' Diary","httpStatus":200},{"type":"openrouter:web_fetch","id":"st_tmp_a5kp6jg9eqa","status":"completed","url":"https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/discussion/5456479/the-i-quit-sugar-by-any-method-support-thread","content":"The \"I Quit Sugar\" (by any method) support thread — MoneySavingExpert Forum\n\nWe’d like to remind Forumites to please avoid political debate on the Forum.\n\nThis is to keep it a safe and useful space for MoneySaving discussions. Threads that are – or become – political in nature may be removed in line with the Forum’s rules. Thank you for your understanding.\n\n### PLEASE READ BEFORE POSTING\n\nHello Forumites! However well-intentioned, for the safety of other users we ask that you refrain from seeking or offering medical advice. This includes recommendations for medicines, procedures or over-the-counter remedies. Posts or threads found to be in breach of this rule will be removed.\n\n📨 Have you signed up to the Forum's new Email Digest yet? Get a selection of trending threads sent straight to your inbox daily, weekly or monthly!\n\n# The \"I Quit Sugar\" (by any method) support thread\n\nPosts: 34,269 Forumite\n\n2 May 2016 at 9:45AM edited 10 May 2016 at 4:50PM in Old style MoneySaving\n\nI gave up processed sugar on 21st April 2016, after over 40 years of an appalling diet. There have been [STRIKE]some[/STRIKE] many days when I have gone to bed realising that all I have eaten is processed sugar (usually in the form of chocolate, biscuits, cake and fruit), especially when busy with work,Meath a vague commitment to \"try harder tomorrow\". So I was surprised that I got through one day, let alone have reached day 12 (although have had a little sugar in the form of bananas, pears and clementines). This is where a bit of help is needed, I am now suffering from sugar withdrawal and it feels awful. I am aware that there is another thread on the health / beauty board, but it's degenerated into a debate about diabetes, with a number of non-evidence based claims made. This thread is primarily about support, encouragement and helping each other through it in an OS-fashion, especially when the cravings hit. Does anyone wish to join me?\n\nIf you haven’t already, join the forum to reply! [purplesignup][/purplesignup]\n\nValue-for-money-for-me-puhleeze!\"No man is worth, crawling on the earth\"- adapted from Bob Crewe and Bob GaudioHope is not a strategy...A child is for life, not just 18 years....Don't get me started on the NHS, because you won't win...I love chaz-ing!\n\n0\n\n## Comments\n\nPosts: 1,365 Forumite\n\n2 May 2016 at 9:54AM\n\nnot quite the same but i have quit saccharin i now use brown sugar that has added stevia and have adjusted to that (you can buy it in tesco) hope that may help someone as i don't think saccharin is good for you either really\n\n0\n\nPosts: 34,269 Forumite\n\n2 May 2016 at 9:55AM\n\nnot quite the same but i have quit saccharin i now use brown sugar that has added stevia and have adjusted to that (you can buy it in tesco) hope that may help someone as i don't think saccharin is good for you either really\n\nAgreed - so I haven't used any sugar substitutes, other than fresh fruit.\n\nValue-for-money-for-me-puhleeze!\"No man is worth, crawling on the earth\"- adapted from Bob Crewe and Bob GaudioHope is not a strategy...A child is for life, not just 18 years....Don't get me started on the NHS, because you won't win...I love chaz-ing!\n\n0\n\nPosts: 1,365 Forumite\n\n2 May 2016 at 9:59AM\n\nits been a big thing for me really, i must drink at least 5 cups of coffee a day all with 2 saccharins in (i never thought i'd manage it)\n\n0\n\nPosts: 195 Forumite\n\n2 May 2016 at 10:01AM\n\nWow, I admire you for doing that. This is an area I need to address also, but I'm going to need to go about it gently. So for now I'm just trying to reduce the amount of coke I drink. Good luck and I'll join you properly in the future, when I've a better handle on things xx\n\nNo one can make you feel inferior without your consent - Eleanor Roosevelt May grocery challenge £7.58 / £200 May no spend days: 1st , 2nd, 3rd\n\n0\n\nPosts: 6,858 Forumite\n\n2 May 2016 at 11:03AM\n\nHi VfM, thank you for the thread - I had looked at the one on the health board and shied away. I'm intending to join you with a couple of personal caveats: 1. Fruit sugar is 'good' 2. Carbs (hidden sugars) are fine in proportion but 'brown' versions should be preferred where available 3. Bread is bad... I can't eat it in proportion, so I'm going to try and just not eat it. This extends to anything with flour in that isn't pasta. 4. The cake is a lie Again, all that is for me. I've not bought anything sugar based (cake, choc, crisps) for a few months, but I've been giving in at work because it's been someones birthday or I've just not had anything else handy to eat with me (hence the fruit). Trying fresh from tomorrow.\n\nThat sounds like a classic case of premature extrapolation. House Bought July 2020 - 19 years 0 months remaining on term Next Step: Bathroom renovation booked for January 2021 Goal: Keep the bigger picture in mind...\n\n0\n\nPosts: 6,530 Forumite\n\n2 May 2016 at 11:34AM\n\nI'm sugar and carb free due to diabetes, if you're struggling to make a favourite meal sugar free, just as as I may be able to help.\n\n0\n\nPosts: 3,479 Forumite\n\n2 May 2016 at 1:27PM\n\nSeeing this thread reminded me that it's nearly 30 years since I gave up sugar. It was in 1987 when I was 22. I used to love sugar, and ate loads of it. However, I had really bad zits, which I despaired of. I tried loads of things for the zits, even two courses of Roacutane. My biggest improvement though was when it occurred to me to give up sugar. I just substituted Candarel for sugar in drinks & on cereal, and gave up chocolate, cakes and sweets. Yes, I know Candarel isn't very good for you either. However I've been using it every day since 1987, and have no ill effects as far as I know. The difference in my skin since giving up sugar was the best incentive possible to stay off it.\n\n0\n\nPosts: 34,269 Forumite\n\n2 May 2016 at 2:53PM edited 2 May 2016 at 2:55PM\n\nHi VfM, thank you for the thread - I had looked at the one on the health board and shied away. I'm intending to join you\n\nGreat, I don't feel quite so alone now :T\n\nwith a couple of personal caveats: 1. Fruit sugar is 'good'\n\nI don't view it as good per se but definitely far better than processed foods. Today I made a banana \"bread\", all the sugar came from ripe bananas, carrots, and some tiny Greek currents, using wholemeal bread flour, oil and baking soda. It's got the texture of cake, but is only just sweet. Everyone seemed to love it and half a big tray (3lb of ingredients) has disappeared already with lunch. Made out of desperation as I was going crazy, not for the actual taste of anything processed, but because I feel v out of sorts atm, which I put down to the lack of sugar.\n\n2. Carbs (hidden sugars) are fine in proportion but 'brown' versions should be preferred where available\n\nAgreed, I knew I would have to substitute rather than cut out although I also know that carb consumption must be highly moderated. It's meant I've eaten a lot more dairy protein and fat.\n\n3. Bread is bad... I can't eat it in proportion, so I'm going to try and just not eat it. This extends to anything with flour in that isn't pasta.\n\nI haven't deprived myself of anything but again agree that carb begets carb. So whilst I haven't had a huge amount of bread, I found treating myself to a couple of croissants (can't stop at one, but around 350kcals) or 2 Weetabix / Oatibix with full fat milk, again around 250kcal. I've also allowed myself crisps (not something I eat regularly) but a 25g pack is nowhere near as bad as a load of chocolate or biscuits. I've also ended up eating 4-5 times a day, meals of 250-350kcal, which is a lot of food for me.\n\n4. The cake is a lie Again, all that is for me. I've not bought anything sugar based (cake, choc, crisps) for a few months, but I've been giving in at work because it's been someones birthday or I've just not had anything else handy to eat with me (hence the fruit). Trying fresh from tomorrow.\n\nYou are so right, not buying it is the best policy! Along with stocking up with plan Bs.\n\nValue-for-money-for-me-puhleeze!\"No man is worth, crawling on the earth\"- adapted from Bob Crewe and Bob GaudioHope is not a strategy...A child is for life, not just 18 years....Don't get me started on the NHS, because you won't win...I love chaz-ing!\n\n0\n\nPosts: 7,729 Forumite\n\n2 May 2016 at 2:55PM\n\nI think that thread over on the H&B board was mine It went really well, i lost weight. But i felt so awful psysically, eating so much meat and dairy. It actually prompted me to go vegan (unintentionally!) because i felt so greasy and full of mucus. But if you are used to eating loads of eggs, meat and dairy,i suggest giving it a go. The cravings (for me at least) was a KILLER for the 1st 3 weeks! :eek:\n\n£10k of Debt Debt Free Diary https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/showthread.php?t=6026621\n\n0\n\nPosts: 6,823 Forumite\n\n2 May 2016 at 4:01PM\n\nI'm in. It's come at the right time too. I'm verging on my second period of disturbing acid reflux and really, really need to get rid of this unhealthy diet I gravitate to every time I get better. I am having manuka honey at the moment and fruit but other sugars are very much off the radar. Hopefully with support it willbe easier.\n\n0\n\nThis discussion has been closed.\n\nBack to Top\n\n★ ★ ★ Meet your Ambassadors\n\n## Confirm your email address to Create Threads and Reply\n\n## 🚀 Getting Started\n\nHi new member!\n\nOur Getting Started Guide will help you get the most out of the Forum\n\n#### Categories\n\n- All Categories\n- 354.9K Banking & Borrowing\n- 254.6K Reduce Debt & Boost Income\n- 455.7K Spending & Discounts\n- 247.7K Work, Benefits & Business\n- 604.7K Mortgages, Homes & Bills\n- 178.7K Life & Family\n- 262.4K Travel & Transport\n- 1.5M Hobbies & Leisure\n- 16.1K Discuss & Feedback\n- 37.7K Read-Only Boards\n\n## Is this how you want to be seen?\n\nWe see you are using a default avatar. It takes only a few seconds to pick a picture.\n\nUpdate Your Picture\n\n## Page Links\n\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/categories/old-style-moneysaving\n- https://www.moneysavingexpert.com/site/forum-faqs/\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/discussion/6612556/new-feature-get-sent-trending-forum-threads-by-email?utm_source=banner\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/profile/VfM4meplse\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/discussion/5399049\n- http://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/register.php\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/discussion/5456479/the-i-quit-sugar-by-any-method-support-thread/p2\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/discussion/5456479/the-i-quit-sugar-by-any-method-support-thread/p3\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/discussion/5456479/the-i-quit-sugar-by-any-method-support-thread/p4\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/discussion/5456479/the-i-quit-sugar-by-any-method-support-thread/p5\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/discussion/5456479/the-i-quit-sugar-by-any-method-support-thread/p6\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/discussion/5456479/the-i-quit-sugar-by-any-method-support-thread/p7\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/discussion/5456479/the-i-quit-sugar-by-any-method-support-thread/p29\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/profile/whiteguineapig\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/discussion/comment/70600889/#Comment_70600889\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/discussion/comment/70600898/#Comment_70600898\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/discussion/comment/70600918/#Comment_70600918\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/profile/cheel\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/discussion/comment/70600925/#Comment_70600925\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/profile/NewShadow\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/discussion/comment/70601262/#Comment_70601262\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/profile/GwylimT\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/discussion/comment/70601452/#Comment_70601452\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/profile/jk0\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/discussion/comment/70601997/#Comment_70601997\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/discussion/comment/70602400/#Comment_70602400\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/profile/WantToBeSE\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/discussion/comment/70602411/#Comment_70602411\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/showthread.php?t=6026621\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/profile/fuddle\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/discussion/comment/70602689/#Comment_70602689\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/discussion/6667256/forum-ambassadors-list-2026-2027\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/start?utm_source=newbie-sidepanel-pocket\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/categories\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/categories/read-only-boards\n- https://forums.moneysavingexpert.com/profile/picture\n","title":"The \"I Quit Sugar\" (by any method) support thread — MoneySavingExpert Forum","httpStatus":200},{"type":"openrouter:web_fetch","id":"st_tmp_7iftuedziv","status":"completed","url":"https://www.soberrecovery.com/forums/newcomers-recovery/466812-am-i-addicted-sugar.html","content":"Am I addicted to sugar? - SoberRecovery : Alcoholism Drug Addiction Help and Information\n\nSoberRecovery : Alcoholism Drug Addiction Help and Information> New to Addiction and Recovery?\n\n>\n\n# Am I addicted to sugar?\n\nUsername\n\nRemember Me?\n\nPassword\n\nNotices\n\nWelcome to the newcomer's forum! We're glad you're here.\n\nAre you looking for drug or alcohol treatment options? Most places accept insurance, provide financing options, or can help you find other funding. Click here for help. You are not alone!\n\nGo to Page...\n\n# Am I addicted to sugar?\n\nPage 1 of 2\n\n1\n\n12-08-2024, 11:56 PM\n\n# 1(permalink)\n\nMember\n\nThread Starter\n\nJoin Date: Dec 2024\n\nPosts: 1\n\nAm I addicted to sugar?\n\n---\n\nI am lost as to why I can't control my food intake. I try. I'm feeling down because it's not severe. I can't find a reason to stop this behaviour. Deciding to quit sugar is too sad. A more lonely life. But my weight is climbing back up. My brain is foggy.\n\nArdent64\n\nFind More Posts by Ardent64\n\n12-09-2024, 12:33 AM\n\n# 2(permalink)\n\nAdministrator\n\nJoin Date: Apr 2007\n\nLocation: Australia\n\nPosts: 222,772\n\nHi and welcome Ardent Our chat room is not open at the moment. You posted in our chat admin forum, so I moved your post here to Newcomers to help find you more response. I believe you can be addicted to sugar, yes. It’s the same dopamine hit that some of us get from alcohol or other drugs. I also believe a lot of us ‘eat our feelings’ to go from being sad to happy again. About a year ago I was diagnosed with Type 2 diabetes. Had to give up sugar. I‘d painted a mental picture in my head about what a deprivation a no sugar diet would be. In fact I didn’t miss it as much as I assumed I would, and no one was any more or less interested in my no sugar journey than they’d be if I’d gone vegan or announced I was lactose intolerant. A no sugar life needn’t be sad or lonely. D\n\nDee74\n\nFind More Posts by Dee74\n\n12-09-2024, 02:30 AM\n\n# 3(permalink)\n\nMember\n\nJoin Date: Nov 2015\n\nLocation: East Coast USA\n\nPosts: 1,418\n\nYes absolutely. I have a close family member who is in the OA fellowship. Thanks to her I live a sugar free life and don't have any type 2 diabetes issues unlike many of my relatives. IME sugar addiction isn't as bad as alcoholism but it's better when one gives up both. One added benefit for me is that several years after giving up the booze and sugar I have lost over 20 pounds without ever dieting.\n\nAAPJ\n\nFind More Posts by AAPJ\n\n12-09-2024, 03:17 AM\n\n# 4(permalink)\n\nMember\n\nJoin Date: Nov 2023\n\nLocation: United Kingdom\n\nPosts: 2,083\n\nYuuup. Sugar’s a killer. I actually think that having a lot of sugar as a kid (I did) can potentially start to map out pathways in the brain that contribute to other addictions later in life, just because of the way it affects our dopamine system…(obviously lots of other factors are involved - genetics, environment, etc). I can’t tell anyone what they should do, but I’ll tell you what I did. When I stopped drinking, I also adopted the keto diet. It made a massive difference in how I felt physically and mentally. The beginning is TOUGH if you are a sugar addict, there’s no two ways about it. But once I got over the initial hump, it was like my body and brain/energy levels were doing what they were always supposed to do. Incredible mental clarity and focus. No sugar cravings. Very few physical alcohol cravings (psychological cravings were still there, but I think with me that was always the harder nut to crack) and no highs or crashes after eating. Just a constant, very steady stream of clean energy. My erm… ‘toilet movements’ were pretty much perfect 😂😂 ‘Award winning poo’s’ … As my body was able to use everything I put into it rather than having to process a load of shite I didn’t need, dragging me down. Combined with exercise, I’m not bragging, but I am now ‘feminine-ripped’. I do cardio, weights, kickboxing and dancing and my body has never looked better! I look better at 41 than I did at 21. I have tons of energy when working out since I have adapted to this way of eating. I was super strict with it at first, but as time has gone on, I do allow myself icecream/burgers occasionally as a treat. But then I go straight back to keto and my body and brain love me for it. My hormones are more balanced than they have ever been. Still not perfect, but the best they have been, so I’m grateful for that. Some people think keto is all sausages, bacon, cheese and double cream. I have a little of that, but mainly salmon, loads of veg, nuts, oils, butter, steak. I love this way of eating, it makes me feel alive and healthy. Obviously do your own research, there’s a lot of naysayers out there about the keto diet, but for me, it’s been transformative. It lowers inflammation in the body specifically the brain (loads of clinical studies out there on this - Google it) and I was even able to come off my antidepressants successfully for the first time in 18 years after about 4 months of simultaneously quitting alcohol and doing the keto diet. (Again, I must stress: This was just what I did. I’m not telling people they have to even aim for coming off their antidepressants. I would have been dead without them when I needed them for all those years. Talk to your doctor etc). Like with any addiction - if you remove the source, you will stop craving it. Keto did this for me with sugar and with alcohol to some degree. Hope you found some of this helpful. Hugs! xxx\n\nKneePads\n\nFind More Posts by KneePads\n\n12-13-2024, 05:48 PM\n\n# 5(permalink)\n\nMember\n\nJoin Date: Nov 2015\n\nLocation: East Coast USA\n\nPosts: 1,418\n\nKneePads nailed it. Similar experience. I'm older, male and probably not as ripped as her because I don't exercise as much but overall the keto diet really helped me improve my health as she described.\n\nAAPJ\n\nFind More Posts by AAPJ\n\n12-13-2024, 06:12 PM\n\n# 6(permalink)\n\nMember\n\nJoin Date: Sep 2013\n\nLocation: SF Bay Area, CA\n\nPosts: 37,021\n\nI just found out I am pre diabetes. I started drinking sugary Cokes last year. It only took a year to become pre diabetic! I am off sugar as much as I can be, I slip sometimes. I have to have another blood test in a little while and see a Hematologist. I think sugar is very, very addictive. When I first gave it up, I wanted to drink or eat candy. It gets better every day. There is an older book called, Sugar Blues, that is very enlightening regarding what sugar can do to one's body. A friend of mine who has been sober a long time told me that when she was drinking she was pre diabetic. Shows how much sugar is in alcohol. Good luck. Good luck!\n\nAlysheba\n\nFind More Posts by Alysheba\n\n12-13-2024, 06:50 PM\n\n# 7(permalink)\n\nAlcohol Abuse Survivor\n\nJoin Date: Oct 2024\n\nPosts: 3,441\n\nQuote:\n\nOriginally Posted by KneePads\n\nWhen I stopped drinking, I also adopted the keto diet. It made a massive difference in how I felt physically and mentally. The beginning is TOUGH if you are a sugar addict, there’s no two ways about it. But once I got over the initial hump, it was like my body and brain/energy levels were doing what they were always supposed to do. Incredible mental clarity and focus. No sugar cravings. Very few physical alcohol cravings (psychological cravings were still there, but I think with me that was always the harder nut to crack) and no highs or crashes after eating. Just a constant, very steady stream of clean energy. My erm… ‘toilet movements’ were pretty much perfect 😂😂 ‘Award winning poo’s’ … As my body was able to use everything I put into it rather than having to process a load of shite I didn’t need, dragging me down. Combined with exercise, I’m not bragging, but I am now ‘feminine-ripped’. I do cardio, weights, kickboxing and dancing and my body has never looked better! I look better at 41 than I did at 21. I have tons of energy when working out since I have adapted to this way of eating\n\nYou are allowed to be proud of your exercise and other (poo) achievements ☺️\n\nRisenShine\n\nFind More Posts by RisenShine\n\n12-13-2024, 06:53 PM\n\n# 8(permalink)\n\nAlcohol Abuse Survivor\n\nJoin Date: Oct 2024\n\nPosts: 3,441\n\nQuote:\n\nOriginally Posted by Alysheba\n\nI just found out I am pre diabetes. I started drinking sugary Cokes last year. It only took a year to become pre diabetic! I am off sugar as much as I can be, I slip sometimes. I have to have another blood test in a little while and see a Hematologist. I think sugar is very, very addictive. When I first gave it up, I wanted to drink or eat candy. It gets better every day. There is an older book called, Sugar Blues, that is very enlightening regarding what sugar can do to one's body. A friend of mine who has been sober a long time told me that when she was drinking she was pre diabetic. Shows how much sugar is in alcohol. Good luck!\n\nI hope you can turn the pre-diabetes around Aly. A friend of mind did by completely cutting out sugar and fasting, but it took dedicated according to her. All the best staying off the sugar\n\nRisenShine\n\nFind More Posts by RisenShine\n\n12-13-2024, 07:00 PM\n\n# 9(permalink)\n\nMember\n\nJoin Date: Oct 2024\n\nPosts: 997\n\nSince I stopped drinking I’ve definitely had sugar cravings. I don’t think they’ve resulted in a higher intake though - I’m just more aware of them now. I ate like a pig when I drank, and by the next day I’d mentally omitted it. I try exercise regularly and eat a high protein diet which helps. If I have a few bars of chocolate here and there, so be it. Unless you think your intake is so severe that it’s giving you health problems I wouldn’t worry too much about it, but don’t get me wrong - I think reducing or even quitting processed sugar would be a great thing too. It’s not good for us.\n\nLilBen0\n\nFind More Posts by LilBen0\n\n12-13-2024, 10:12 PM\n\n# 10(permalink)\n\nAlcohol Abuse Survivor\n\nJoin Date: Oct 2024\n\nPosts: 3,441\n\nWhat worked for me was 5% fruit juice + 95% water or dash of lime/lemon juice (I buy pouches of it, I dont buy the actual fruit as its too expensive that way and not in season all year) + 1/4 teaspoon sugar + water (per metric cup). If I made up my 2L container of citrus water then that would equate to 2 teaspoons of sugar in the entire 2L container It's the only way I could get off sugary drinks and it's the only way I can get 2L down me. You could always try this Im not a big fan of sugary food, but Ive had to rein that in a bit lately because since I gave up drinking I started eating more sugary foods, but not lollies/candy, ice cream is my thing\n\nRisenShine\n\nFind More Posts by RisenShine\n\n12-14-2024, 07:10 AM\n\n# 11(permalink)\n\nMember\n\nJoin Date: Nov 2018\n\nPosts: 7,148\n\nI'm probably addicted to sugar. I do restrain myself, but when I do, I crave. And when I do give in, I often go berserk. And processed sugar as opposed to the kind you find in fruit, reacts to your body in a way that is not good for you. Not as bad as alcohol. At least I can say that.\n\nDriGuy\n\nFind More Posts by DriGuy\n\n12-14-2024, 09:06 AM\n\n# 12(permalink)\n\nMember\n\nJoin Date: Sep 2013\n\nLocation: SF Bay Area, CA\n\nPosts: 37,021\n\nThank you so much Risen! So sweet of you to cheer me on. Sugar is hard for me, especially after eating/drinking so much of it the last year. I was totally addicted to sugary Cokes. Bad. It only took a year to become pre diabetic. I am off them completely. I'm trying to drink a high protein drink with only one gram of sugar in it in the morning and it seems to help. They say protein is a blood sugar stabilizer. Anyway, I slip up, but am not eating nearly the sugar I was. Much better for the teeth too. Good luck to those of us struggling with sugar. Sugar Blues is an old book, very interesting about what sugar can do to your body. So really badly for one. Before I started drinking the sugary Cokes, I barely craved sweets at all. Best of luck all. Still waiting for my implant. I'm getting so tired of having a space in my mouth where it belongs. Thankfully it's not super noticeable. I'm so vain about my teeth. The universe will teach you something when you get too vain about somethings! I sure hope the implant is taking this time!\n\nAlysheba\n\nFind More Posts by Alysheba\n\n12-14-2024, 09:09 AM\n\n# 13(permalink)\n\nMember\n\nJoin Date: Feb 2019\n\nLocation: The Midwest\n\nPosts: 679\n\nQuote:\n\nOriginally Posted by Ardent64\n\nI am lost as to why I can't control my food intake. I try. I'm feeling down because it's not severe. I can't find a reason to stop this behaviour. Deciding to quit sugar is too sad. A more lonely life. But my weight is climbing back up. My brain is foggy.\n\nI recommend trying Whole30. Sometimes it help to do a 30 day \"reset,\" just shop the outside of the story. Replace your candy with fresh fruit and berries. Replace your snacks with veggies. Be creative with your cooking - look at every label. Then when you have candy again, keep the habit of having the good foods with it. Personally, I love candy! I also love to cook and enjoy all those things. I also do a lot of fasting/IF. Health is a journey, keep studying/researching and see what works for you -- but Whole30, for me, was a great first s